sources:
    - id: henrich2005
      title: '"Economic man" in cross-cultural perspective: Behavioral experiments in 15 small-scale societies'
      authors: J. Henrich, R. Boyd, S. Bowles, C. Camerer, E. Fehr, H. Gintis, R. McElreath et al.
      year: 2005
      finding: Ultimatum game in 15 small-scale societies (Behavioral and Brain Sciences 28:795–855). Mean offers from 25–26% (Quichua, Machiguenga) to 57% (Lamalera); Machiguenga mode 15%, 1 of 21 offers rejected, stake ≈ 2.3 days' wages; first Machiguenga result in 1996.
    - id: henrich2001
      title: 'In search of Homo economicus: behavioral experiments in 15 small-scale societies'
      authors: J. Henrich, R. Boyd, S. Bowles, C. Camerer, E. Fehr, H. Gintis, R. McElreath
      year: 2001
      finding: 'American Economic Review 91(2): mean ultimatum offers across the 15 societies ranged from 26% to 58%; group differences tracked payoffs to cooperation and market integration.'
    - id: camerer2003
      title: Behavioral Game Theory
      authors: C. F. Camerer
      year: 2003
      finding: In standard ultimatum experiments in industrialised societies, mean offers are typically 40–50% of the pie; Roth et al. (1991) found little variation across Pittsburgh, Ljubljana, Jerusalem and Tokyo.

    label n_mach "Machiguenga" below attach_to bar_2 font_size 20 hidden true
    label n_gnau "Gnau" below attach_to bar_8 font_size 20 hidden true
    label n_au "Au" below attach_to bar_10 font_size 20 hidden true
    label n_lam "Lamalera" below attach_to bar_15 font_size 20 hidden true
    text rules "Offer → accept, or refuse: both get 0" #8f887c x 500 y 625 font_size 30 hidden true
    portrait henrich x 820 y 560 width 170 of "Joseph Henrich" cameo true hidden true
    text mode "most common: 15%\n1 of 21 refused" #b5482e cites ["henrich2005"] x 440 y 545 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Fifteen societies"

Does where you grow up change how you split money with a stranger?
    draw axes

B: Take the ultimatum game: one player (@draw rules@) offers a share of a pot, the other accepts it, or refuses and both get nothing.

With students, from Pittsburgh to Tokyo, the average offer sit (@draw reference_1@)s between forty and fifty percent, and barely mo (@highlight reference_1@)ves from country to country.

    erase rules

B: So it looked like human nature. Then in 1996 a young anthropologist, J (@draw henrich@)oseph Henrich, played it with the Machiguenga, forest farmers in the Peruvian Amazon.

    erase henrich

    ask "Drag the Machiguenga bar to your guess: their average offer, in % of the pot." on bar_2 store g default "45"
        intro: The pot was about two days' wages.
        right: Twenty-six percent: close to your {g}.
        wrong: You said {g}: it was twenty-six.

    draw n_mach

Twenty-six percent. The most common offer was fift (@draw mode@)een, and only one offer in twenty-one was refused.

    pause 0.3

B: A fluke of one village, or something real?
    highlight bar_2

    erase mode

A team of anthropologists and economists  (@draw bar_1 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 bar_6 bar_7 bar_8 bar_9 bar_10 bar_11 bar_12 bar_13 bar_14 bar_15 n_gnau n_au n_lam parallel true@)went to find out, in fifteen small societies, from the Amazon to Mongolia to Papua New Guinea.

    text fifteen "15 societies, 4 continents" #8f887c cites ["henrich2005"] x 500 y 625 font_size 30

B: At the top, the whale hunters of Lamalera in Indonesia: fifty-seven percent, more than half the pot.
    highlight bar_15

    erase fifteen

So the student average was never human nature. It was one kind of society's answer, and the range runs from a quarter to over half.
    highlight reference_1

## Cooperation and offers
