sources:
    - id: henrich2005
      title: '"Economic man" in cross-cultural perspective: Behavioral experiments in 15 small-scale societies'
      authors: J. Henrich, R. Boyd, S. Bowles, C. Camerer, E. Fehr, H. Gintis, R. McElreath et al.
      year: 2005
      finding: Behavioral and Brain Sciences 28:795–855. Ethnographers' rankings of payoffs to cooperation (1–7) and aggregate market integration both predict group mean ultimatum offers; together they explain 47% (adjusted R²) of the between-society variance. Age, sex and wealth predicted little; group membership predicted a lot. Au and Gnau rejected offers above 50% about as often as offers below; the authors link this to Melanesian gift-giving, where accepting a gift creates an obligation. Lamalera's whale catch is divided by fixed rules, 'similar to dividing a whale'.

    text grp "Who you are: little\nYour group: a lot" #2f6b8f cites ["henrich2005"] x 360 y 545 font_size 32 hidden true
    text mkt "Markets: higher offers too" #2f6b8f cites ["henrich2005"] x 360 y 555 font_size 30 hidden true
    text half "≈ half the gap explained" #2f6b8f cites ["henrich2005"] x 360 y 500 font_size 30 hidden true
    text gift "Refused offers over 50%" #b5482e cites ["henrich2005"] x 360 y 555 font_size 30 hidden true
    text debt "big gift = a debt" #b5482e cites ["henrich2005"] x 360 y 505 font_size 30 hidden true
    path fitA thick #7b4fa0 cites ["henrich2005"] points [[110,277],[930,516]] hidden true
    path fitB dashed thick #7b4fa0 points [[110,295],[930,456]] hidden true

    card title "Cooperation and offers"

B: So the offers differ by society. And it isn't the individuals (@draw grp@): age, sex and wealth predicted almost nothing. Which group you belonged to predicted a lot.

Then what is it about the group?
    highlight grp

    erase grp

B: The team ranked each society on how much people gain from cooperating beyond their own family.
    draw axes

B: Machiguenga families mos (@draw point_1@)tly provide for themselves: rank one. Lamalera's whale  (@draw point_2@)crews are big teams of non-kin: rank seven.

So a split in the game looks like splitting a whale?
    highlight point_2

B: Their words, almost. Here are al (@draw point_3 point_4 points parallel true@)l fifteen: the more a society lives by coo (@draw fitA@)peration, the higher its offers.

B: A second ranking, how mu (@draw mkt@)ch people buy and sell in markets, points the same way.

Together the two rankings account (@draw half@) for about half the differences between societies.

    erase mkt half

    ask "Which named society pulls the line up most? Tap it." choose ["point_1","point_2","point_3","point_4"] answer point_2
        right: Lamalera: high offers, far out on the right, so it tilts the line up most.
        wrong: Look for the named dot that is both high and far to the right: here, Lamalera.

    fade fitA to 0.3

Try one change: drop Lamale (@animate stage 1 duration 2.5@)ra, the highest point. The (@draw fitB@) line flattens, but it still climbs.

    erase fitB

    fade fitA to 1

    animate stage 0 duration 1

Now the str (@draw gift@)angest pair: the Au and Gnau of Papua New Guinea. They often refused offers of more than half.
    highlight point_3 point_4

B: The researchers point to local custom: accepting a (@draw debt@) big gift puts you in debt to the giver. Refusing it refuses the obligation.

    erase gift debt

So the game never measured one fairness instinct. People played it by the rules of their own everyday life.
    highlight fitA

## Culture, measured
