sources:
    - id: gsz2006
      title: Does culture affect economic outcomes?
      authors: L. Guiso, P. Sapienza, L. Zingales
      year: 2006
      finding: 'Journal of Economic Perspectives 20(2): defines culture narrowly as customary beliefs and values that ethnic, religious and social groups transmit fairly unchanged from generation to generation, so that its effect on economic outcomes can be tested.'
    - id: fm2007
      title: 'Corruption, norms, and legal enforcement: evidence from diplomatic parking tickets'
      authors: R. Fisman, E. Miguel
      year: 2007
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 115(6). UN diplomats in New York, immune from parking fines, Nov 1997–Oct 2002: unpaid violations per diplomat per year Kuwait 246, Egypt 140, Pakistan 69, Nigeria 59, Brazil 30; Norway, Canada, Japan 0. Violations track home-country corruption. After enforcement in late 2002 (towing, loss of permits) violations fell sharply; the cross-country pattern weakened but persisted.'
    - id: agn2013
      title: 'On the origins of gender roles: women and the plough'
      authors: A. Alesina, P. Giuliano, N. Nunn
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Quarterly Journal of Economics 128(2): descendants of societies that traditionally used the plough have less equal gender attitudes and lower female participation in work, politics and business today, including among children of immigrants.'

    scratch def work ["Culture = beliefs + values,","handed down","Test: same rules, different heritage"] x 500 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    text years "New York, 1997–2002 · no penalty" #8f887c cites ["fm2007"] x 420 y 585 font_size 28 hidden true
    text corr "tracks corruption at home" #b5482e cites ["fm2007"] x 380 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    text tow "Late 2002: towing" #2f6b8f cites ["fm2007"] x 380 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true
    text fell "→ tickets fell sharply" #2f6b8f cites ["fm2007"] x 380 y 415 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon plough size 190 set twemoji x 500 y 480 or ["plough","tractor"] icon_look picture of plow hidden true
    text women "plough ancestors → fewer women at work" #b5482e cites ["agn2013"] x 500 y 330 font_size 34 hidden true
    text immig "even immigrants' daughters" #3d3833 cites ["agn2013"] x 500 y 265 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Culture, measured"

People carry their home rules into a game. Economists call th (@draw def_box def_line_1@)at baggage culture, but they had to pin the word down first.

B: Luigi Guiso, Paola Sapienza and Luigi Zingales did, in 2006: beliefs and values that groups (@draw def_line_2@) hand down, fairly unchanged, from generation to generation.

A norm, like a tip, is one such rule; culture is the whole kit. To test it, find (@draw def_line_3@) people with different heritage under the same rules.

    erase def

B: New York supplied them by accident. D (@draw axes years@)iplomats at the United Nations had immunity: they could park illegally and never pay.

Same city, same streets, same zero penalty. Ray Fisman and Edward Miguel counted the unpaid tickets.
    highlight years

From 1997 to 2002, diplomat (@draw bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 parallel true@)s from Norway, Canada and Japan ran up none at all.

B: Kuwai (@draw bar_4 bar_5 bar_6 bar_7@)t's ran up (@draw bar_8@) about two hundred and forty-six each, a year. Egypt's, about a hundred and forty.

    erase years

And the ranking tracked how corrupt the (@draw corr@) home country is rated. A norm from home, kept where nobody enforced it.

    erase corr

B: Then, in late 2002, New York (@draw tow@) won the right to tow their cars and strip their parking permits.

Unpaid tickets f (@draw fell@)ell sharply. The gaps between countries shrank, but didn't vanish.

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 bar_6 bar_7 bar_8 tow fell

B: Culture can also reach back centuries. Where anc (@draw plough@)estors farmed with the plough, heavy work that favoured men's strength, women t (@draw women@)oday work outside the home less.

Alberto Alesina, Paola Giuliano and Nathan Nunn found it even am (@draw immig@)ong immigrants' daughters in America and Europe: same laws, inherited beliefs.

## Culture or rules?
sources:
    - id: henrich2005
      title: '"Economic man" in cross-cultural perspective: Behavioral experiments in 15 small-scale societies'
      authors: J. Henrich et al.
      year: 2005
      finding: Group mean ultimatum offers rose with market integration and payoffs to cooperation; the authors note they cannot tell the direction of causality.
    - id: fm2007
      title: 'Corruption, norms, and legal enforcement: evidence from diplomatic parking tickets'
      authors: R. Fisman, E. Miguel
      year: 2007
      finding: Unpaid diplomatic parking violations in New York fell sharply after enforcement began in late 2002.
    - id: hhn2010
      title: The weirdest people in the world?
      authors: J. Henrich, S. J. Heine, A. Norenzayan
      year: 2010
      finding: 'Behavioral and Brain Sciences 33: in top psychology journals 2003–2007, 96% of subjects came from Western industrialised countries, which hold about 12% of the world''s population (Arnett 2008); WEIRD samples are often outliers.'

    box cult "Culture" #2f6b8f x 220 y 480 width 260 height 110 radius 12 font_size 38 hidden true
    box inst "Institutions" #b5482e x 780 y 480 width 280 height 110 radius 12 font_size 38 hidden true
    arrow a_ci curved #2f6b8f from.x 360 from.y 510 to.x 630 to.y 510 hidden true
    arrow a_ic curved #b5482e from.x 630 from.y 450 to.x 360 to.y 450 hidden true
    text mkt "markets → higher offers?" #b5482e cites ["henrich2005"] x 500 y 360 font_size 30 hidden true
    text tow "towing → habits change in months" #b5482e cites ["fm2007"] x 500 y 305 font_size 30 hidden true
    text trust "rules need trust" #2f6b8f x 500 y 600 font_size 30 hidden true
    text grow "Why nations grow rich: still argued" #8f887c x 500 y 235 font_size 30 hidden true
    text hhn "Henrich, Heine & Norenzayan, 2010" #8f887c cites ["hhn2010"] x 500 y 610 font_size 28 hidden true
    text weird "W · E · I · R · D" #3d3833 cites ["hhn2010"] x 500 y 555 font_size 64 hidden true
    text weird2 "Western · Educated · Industrialised · Rich · Democratic" #8f887c x 500 y 495 font_size 28 hidden true
    text big "96% of samples" #b5482e cites ["hhn2010"] x 500 y 400 font_size 60 hidden true
    text big2 "from 12% of humanity" #3d3833 cites ["hhn2010"] x 500 y 330 font_size 40 hidden true
    text corner "students = one corner" #2f6b8f x 500 y 250 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s1 "Culture shifts choices: mostly agreed" #4f7a3a x 500 y 470 font_size 38 hidden true
    text s2 "How much, vs rules: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 385 font_size 38 hidden true
    text close2 "culture, or the rules?" #b5482e x 500 y 330 font_size 36 hidden true
    text close "Same game, same money:\nsplit like home" #2f6b8f x 500 y 440 font_size 46 hidden true

    card title "Culture or rules?"

B: But which wa (@draw cult inst a_ic@)y does it run? Offers rose with market integration. Maybe ma (@draw mkt@)rkets made the culture, not the other way round.

And towing changed  (@draw tow@)diplomats' habits within months. Rules can beat inherited norms, and fast.

B: Yet rules only work if  (@draw a_ci trust@)people trust them. Culture and institutions shape each other, and which matters more for w (@draw grow@)hy nations grow rich is still argued.

    erase cult inst a_ci a_ic mkt tow trust grow

A second worry is about the lab itself. In (@draw hhn@) 2010 Joseph Henrich, Steven Heine and Ara Norenzayan named the problem: mos (@draw weird@)t subjects are WEIRD.

B: Western, (@draw weird2@) Educated, Industrialised, Rich and Democratic. In top psychology journals, ninety-six perce (@draw big@)nt of samples came from countries with tw (@draw big2@)elve percent of humanity.

And the ultimatum game was the warning: students looked like humanity, until fifteen socie (@draw corner@)ties showed they were one corner of it.

    erase hhn weird weird2 big big2 corner

B: How sure are we? That culture shifts econ (@draw s1@)omic choices, measurably: mostly agreed.

How much it matters nex (@draw s2@)t to institutions, and which drives which: contested.

    quiz "Say a new tax-reporting law covers a whole country. Compliance jumps in one region and barely moves in another. Is that culture?" intro "Three new cases." voice a
        right: Maybe: the shared law holds the rules fixed, so inherited trust is a candidate, but only if enforcement is equal too.
        wrong: Ask what one law holds fixed, and what it doesn't: here, maybe, if enforcement is equal too.
        + Maybe: the law is the same, so inherited trust is a candidate, if enforcement is equal too
        * No: one law, so culture can't matter
        * Certainly: nothing else could differ

    quiz "A firm tests a bonus scheme on its Oslo staff. It works well, so the firm rolls it out to offices in thirty countries. What's the risk?" voice b
        right: One corner: like the student offers, a result from one kind of society may not travel.
        wrong: Remember how the student offers looked universal: here, Oslo staff may be one corner of humanity.
        * None: money works the same everywhere
        + Oslo staff may be one corner of humanity
        * Bonuses only work in Norway

    quiz "Countries where people trust strangers more tend to have cleaner courts. What does that link, on its own, tell you?" voice a
        right: Not which way it runs: culture and institutions shape each other, so the link alone can't say.
        wrong: Recall the arrows between culture and institutions: here, not which way it runs.
        * Trust builds good courts
        * Good courts build trust
        + Not which way it runs

    erase s1 s2

So does culture shape economic choices? The same game and the  (@draw close@)same money, split like the place people come from. How much of that is culture, and how much the rules a (@draw close2@)round it, economics is still finding out.

